<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sang Nila Utama</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Sri Tri Buana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sang Nila Utama is the legendary founder of the Kingdom of Singapura, known from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-annals">Malay Annals</a>. According to the story, this prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a> landed on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temasek">Temasek</a>, saw a beast he took for a lion and named the place Singapura, the 'lion city', around 1299. His dynasty ruled until about 1400, and its last king founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sang-nila-utama.jpg" alt="Sang Nila Utama" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Altopian1 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Sang_Nila_Utama_at_the_Raffles_Landing_Site_(three-quarter_front_view).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>In the Malay Annals Sang Nila Utama is a prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a>, son of King Sang Sapurba, the supposed descendant of Rajendra I through his Islamic guise as Iskandar Zulkarnain and the half-mythical ancestor of many rulers of the Malay world. He married Princess Wan Sri Bini, daughter of Permaisuri Iskandar Syah, queen of Bentan on Bintan Island, and received a jewelled golden crown and a royal signet ring. His title at his coronation, Sri Tri Buana, means Lord of Three Worlds: perhaps the heavens, the human world and the underworld, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temasek">Temasek</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The Annals tell that while hunting on Bintan he chased a stag up a hill, climbed a great rock and saw across the sea an island with a white beach, which his chief minister named as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temasek">Temasek</a>. On the crossing a storm nearly sank his ship until, on the captain's advice, he threw his crown into the sea. Hunting near the river mouth on the open ground now called the Padang, he saw a beast with a red body, black head and white breast, which his chief minister Demang Lebar Daun called a lion. Taking it as a good omen, he founded a city there and named it Singapura.</p><p>He had two sons with Wan Sri Bini. The elder, Raja Kechil-Besar, succeeded him as Sri Wikrama Wira, and the younger became his prime minister. Sang Nila Utama was buried on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-canning-hill">Bukit Larangan</a>, now Fort Canning Hill, though the site of his grave is unknown.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Scholars debate how far the Annals record real events, and some hold that he may be a mythical figure, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>'s 14th-century settlement is no longer in doubt: the Yuan traveller Wang Dayuan described Long Ya Men and Ban Zu under local rulers. The tale is highly symbolic. Casting away the crown may stand for the shift of power from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a> to Singapura, and since lions never lived in Singapore the beast has been read as a Malayan tiger or the janggi of Minangkabau legend. Some suggest the figure was a literary device to cover the story of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parameswara">Parameswara</a>, the founder of Melaka, whose life in the Suma Oriental of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tome-pires">Tomé Pires</a> is strikingly similar.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sang Nila Utama” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Malay Annals tell how <b>Sang Nila Utama</b>, a prince of Palembang, landed on the island of Temasek, saw a beast he took for a lion and named the place Singapura, lion city.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-singapura">Kingdom of Singapura</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/malaysia">Malaysia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-0.3,101.27&amp;year=1264" aria-label="Sumatra on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7363 2175 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sang Nila Utama"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7813 2445h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7813 2445h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-0.3,101.27&amp;year=1264">What was here in 1264? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
